Give razerkbd and razermouse a serial_sha256 attribute

hyperflux-next-bridge leaves out its virtual device while a wired device
of the same model is plugged in, since that is usually the same unit on
its cable. It can't tell a second unit apart: device_serial is readable
only by OpenRazer's group, and the bridge runs as a system user outside
it.

serial_sha256 holds the SHA-256 of the serial that device_serial shows,
as 64 lowercase hex digits, and anyone may read it. NUL and white space
at both ends are trimmed first, as the bridge trims the serial it reads
through the receiver, so the same serial gives the same hash on both
sides. An empty serial, or one with anything but printable ASCII left,
has none, and reading fails with ENODATA. Like device_serial, each read
asks the device.

Only USB devices get it: not the virtual devices, and not Blade laptops,
whose serial comes from DMI and only root may read.

Serials are short and follow a pattern, so trying candidates against the
hash would find one. The hash keeps the serial out of plain sight; it
doesn't make it secret.

Applies to OpenRazer 3.12.4 (1ef8a91712906a89fd332a2ed62bbd105315d6d1)
after 0001 and 0002.

diff --git a/driver/razercommon.c b/driver/razercommon.c
index fc4c271..68b06d4 100644
--- a/driver/razercommon.c
+++ b/driver/razercommon.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/init.h>
 #include <linux/hid.h>
+#include <crypto/sha2.h>
 
 #include "razercommon.h"
 
@@ -424,3 +425,48 @@ int razer_hyperflux_get_report(struct hid_device *hdev, struct razer_report *req
     }
     return 0;
 }
+
+/*
+ * serial_sha256 for hyperflux-next-bridge, which can't read device_serial
+ * (only OpenRazer's group may) and only needs to know whether a wired
+ * device is the unit it reaches through the receiver. buf holds len bytes
+ * that device_serial's show function wrote; they become a hash of the
+ * serial in hex. NUL and white space at both ends go first, exactly as the
+ * bridge trims the serial it reads, and a serial that is then empty or has
+ * anything but printable ASCII gets no hash.
+ *
+ * The hash is over a fixed label and the trimmed serial, so it equals the
+ * fingerprint the bridge makes from the serial it reads through the
+ * receiver and the two can be compared. The label keeps this value from
+ * doubling as a hash of the serial on its own.
+ */
+#define RAZER_SERIAL_HASH_LABEL "hyperflux-next serial"
+
+static bool razer_serial_trim(char c)
+{
+    return !c || c == ' ' || c == '\t' || c == '\n' || c == '\f' || c == '\r';
+}
+
+ssize_t razer_serial_sha256(char *buf, ssize_t len)
+{
+    /* The label with its NUL, then the longest serial either driver reads. */
+    u8 message[sizeof(RAZER_SERIAL_HASH_LABEL) + 64];
+    u8 digest[SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE];
+    ssize_t start = 0, end = len, i;
+    size_t label = sizeof(RAZER_SERIAL_HASH_LABEL);
+
+    while (start < end && razer_serial_trim(buf[start]))
+        start++;
+    while (end > start && razer_serial_trim(buf[end - 1]))
+        end--;
+    if (start == end || (size_t)(end - start) > sizeof(message) - label)
+        return -ENODATA;
+    for (i = start; i < end; i++) {
+        if ((unsigned char)buf[i] < 0x21 || (unsigned char)buf[i] > 0x7e)
+            return -ENODATA;
+    }
+    memcpy(message, RAZER_SERIAL_HASH_LABEL, label);
+    memcpy(message + label, buf + start, end - start);
+    sha256(message, label + (end - start), digest);
+    return sysfs_emit(buf, "%*phN\n", SHA256_DIGEST_SIZE, digest);
+}
